# config/auth.conf — real values. Gitignored; the tracked template is
# config/auth.conf.example.
#
# Used by `dots auth` (lib/auth.sh) to restore the shared SSH key from
# 1Password onto a machine that's never had it, per docs/ssh-setup-plan.md
# Phase 6. This is the one place that key's 1Password location is named —
# change it here, not in lib/auth.sh, if the vault/item ever gets renamed.

# 1Password vault + item holding the SSH key (Phase 6: "Add Private Key" ->
# "Import a Key File", saved as a real SSH Key item — not a password/note).
# lib/auth.sh reads it via `op read "op://<vault>/<item>/private
# key?ssh-format=openssh"` — the ssh-format query param is required (op
# read's plain default for this field is PKCS#8 PEM, which ssh-keygen/
# ssh-add can't use at all — verified 2026-09-19).
#
# IMPORTANT: if your vault's name has characters an op:// reference can't
# parse (confirmed: '&' — and percent-encoding it does NOT work, op
# rejects '%' too), use the vault's ID here instead of its name. Find it
# with `op vault list`. Plain spaces in either name are fine (1Password's
# own docs use "ssh key" with a space as their example).
OP_SSH_KEY_VAULT="Infra"

# >>> CONFIRM: the actual item title in 1Password. Phase 6 suggested
# >>> "MacBook Pro SSH key — GitHub + Homelab" at the time, but that's
# >>> machine-specific wording for a key that's actually shared across
# >>> every machine (GitHub + all 3 Proxmox nodes + every VM/LXC trust the
# >>> same key — see lib/auth.sh's header for why). Worth renaming the item
# >>> in 1Password itself to something machine-agnostic first, e.g.
# >>> "dots SSH key — GitHub + Homelab", then putting that exact title here.
OP_SSH_KEY_ITEM=""
